NS.P2 Open loop under TARGETS 1.1 P2: commands are offered on the frozen schedule and latency runs from the scheduled offer to the published ACK (sent only once the commit is durable and readable), so queueing behind other writers counts. The trace's own commands are reported per size band: the 5-to-10 band (NS-P2-BAND-5-10) and the 11-to-25 band (NS-P2-BAND-11-25) each meet NS-P2-COMMIT-P50-MS and NS-P2-COMMIT-P99-MS, the 26-to-50 band (NS-P2-BAND-26-50) meets NS-P2-COMMIT-P99-26-50-MS, all with P1's 256 readers running; the 10-fact probe is a diagnostic only. Every offered command is committed with a receipt, rejected with a typed error, or a typed conflict (NS-P2-UNACCOUNTED-MAX, NS-P2-ACCOUNTING), and accounting is not progress: every oracle-valid trace command publishes; typed rejections count only for the trace's deliberately invalid commands and typed conflicts only where the oracle independently finds a compare-and-set race (NS-P2-UNJUSTIFIED-OUTCOMES-MAX, NS-P2-PROGRESS); queued commands never exceed NS-P2-QUEUE-MAX-S of the offered rate at steady state (NS-P2-QUEUE-BOUND) and the queue drains within NS-P2-BURST-DRAIN-S after each burst (NS-P2-BURST-DRAIN) (RULINGS Q31a). The pull at the returned tx sees the commit every time with no extra wait (NS-P2-RYOW-FRACTION); duplicates and lost ACKs return the original receipt every time (NS-P2-DUP-RECEIPT-FRACTION).
NS.P3 Under TARGETS 1.1 P3: feed entries carry tx, entity, attribute and the valid-time range affected; commit time is the source's publication instant on the shared host monotonic clock; the cursor is (epoch, position); the expected affected-subject set per transaction is the directly written subjects (O-REPLAY) plus the subjects whose WORLD_W0 static read set contains a changed fact (O-DOMAIN, NS-P3-DOMAIN-AFFECTED-SET, RULINGS Q36e); 'affected subjects known' is the consumer's single durable write of the set and its checkpoint. Over a run there are zero gaps (NS-P3-GAPS-MAX) and zero per-subject order violations (NS-P3-ORDER-VIOLATIONS-MAX); lag stays within NS-P3-LAG-P99-MS with P1's 256 readers running; delivery is at-least-once from the last checkpoint with effects keyed by (consumer, position, subject), so crashes before the effect, after the effect before the checkpoint, and after the checkpoint (NS-P3-CRASH-BEFORE-EFFECT, NS-P3-CRASH-AFTER-EFFECT, NS-P3-CRASH-AFTER-CHECKPOINT) leave zero visible duplicate effects (NS-P3-VISIBLE-DUP-EFFECTS-MAX) while replay attempts are counted separately; resume from a checkpoint replays exactly.
G7.composition.ack-and-routed-ryow Per COMPOSITION ackOwnership (RULINGS Q31e, replacing Q28's ACK clause): the source of truth sends every ACK after its own publication (CO-ACK-AFTER-SOURCE-PUBLICATION, OBS-ACK-ORDER); the pull at the returned position is served by an index eligible for it, else by the fallback, so read-your-own-write is exact (CO-RYOW-ROUTED); during a current-index outage commits and ACKs continue and pulls fall back (CO-OUTAGE-ACKS-CONTINUE), and the index catches up from the feed through a bounded queue (CO-CATCHUP-BOUNDED-QUEUE). Per COMPOSITION fallbackEquivalence (RULINGS Q34b), this holds on every serving surface the composition claims in the lane's language, native included (CO-RYOW-PER-SURFACE); a stale fallback is rejected (CO-STALE-FALLBACK-REJECTED); a cross-language fallback recipe is bound to the same semantic query identity (parameters, basis, meaning) and the same oracle answer (CO-CROSS-LANGUAGE-RECIPE-IDENTITY), with total request latency and language attribution recorded.
G8.composition.watermark-routing Per COMPOSITION eligibility (RULINGS Q31d): an index serves basis B only when its watermark, a fully resolved prefix, has reached B in B's epoch (CO-EPOCH-MISMATCH), C1's conditions hold, it can reconstruct B (a current-only index only inside the validity interval of every attribute read, CO-RECONSTRUCT-OUTSIDE-VALIDITY) and it has applied every published erasure (CO-ERASURE-FRONTIER); a read with no eligible index waits at most T-WATERMARK-WAIT-MS (CO-WATERMARK-WAIT) and then routes to an eligible fallback (CO-WATERMARK-FALLBACK) whose latency counts in the read's own latency (CO-FALLBACK-LATENCY-COUNTS); the receipt records the route and the surface language, a cross-language fallback is counted per surface (CO-FALLBACK-LANGUAGE), and fallbacks are reported as a share of reads per window (CO-FALLBACK-SHARE, OBS-WATERMARK); no index answers for a basis it is not eligible for (CO-NO-STALE-ANSWER); history and analytics trail asynchronously behind their watermarks (CO-ASYNC-HISTORY).
G8.eligibility.c1-routing Every C1-* case of CS-ELIGIBILITY: the observed route (OBS-PLAN-ROUTE) equals the C1 expected route for the whole query, including a partial entity excluded by an early filter but relevant to ordering, negation or aggregation, a mapping-generation mismatch, explicit and decreasing recorded times, interior positions and V outside the marker window; answers equal O-REPLAY.
G10.composition.identity-bound Every receipt binds the four assignments (source, current, history, analytics) with image digest, configuration hash and slot each, every replication edge (feed consumer source hash, translation generation) and the serving index's watermark at every read (CO-RECEIPT-BINDS-ALL, VA14); engine-scope keys derive from the assignment of the obligation's layer, never from engineOfLane (CO-SCOPE-KEY-FROM-ASSIGNMENT).
Composition {'assignments': ['source', 'current', 'history', 'analytics'], 'engines': 'each assignment is XTDB or QLever', 'receiptBinding': "every receipt binds, per assignment, the engine image digest, configuration hash and slot; every replication edge (feed consumer source hash, translation generation); and each serving index's watermark at read time", 'basis': 'a basis is a source-of-truth position (POSITION coordinates (stream, epoch, offset, ordinal)) plus a valid time', 'eligibility': "index I is eligible to serve basis B only when all hold: its watermark (a fully resolved prefix) has reached B in B's epoch; C1's eligibility for the query (mapping, valid time, exact cut); I can actually reconstruct B (a current-only index only when B is at or after the last change to every attribute the read touches, the validity interval of R18); and I has applied every erasure published so far, independently of B (ERASURE frontier) (RULINGS Q31d)", 'watermarkRouting': "a read that finds no eligible index waits at most T-WATERMARK-WAIT-MS for one, then routes to an eligible fallback (the source of truth's own query surface or another index); the fallback's latency counts in the read's own latency; the receipt records the route and the surface language, a cross-language fallback is counted per surface, and fallbacks are reported as a share of reads per window; no index answers for a basis it is not eligible for (RULINGS Q31d)", 'fallbackEquivalence': 'outage read-your-own-write is tested on every serving surface the composition claims, native surfaces included; a stale fallback is rejected; where a fallback answers in another language, both recipes are bound to one semantic query identity (parameters, basis, meaning) and one expected answer from the oracle; total request latency and language attribution stay recorded (RULINGS Q34b)', 'ackOwnership': "the source of truth sends the ACK after its own publication; read-your-own-write holds by routing (the pull at the returned position goes to an eligible index, else the fallback); in normal operation the current index is kept synchronous so the fast path serves that pull; during a current-index outage commits and ACKs continue, pulls fall back, the index catches up from the feed through a bounded queue, and fallback share and recovery time are measured (GS.04b); history and analytics stay asynchronous behind watermarks (RULINGS Q31e, replacing Q28's ACK clause)", 'layerSwaps': 'layers are swapped one at a time under matched workloads at small rungs (current-index candidates on P1 and P4; history candidates on past-basis reads and GS.12; source candidates on P2 and P3); only the two or three best compositions go on to end-to-end validation with the golden scenarios', 'scopeKey': "an engine-scope receipt's key is (layer, the engine assigned to that layer, its image digest, configuration hash and slot), derived from the composition, never from engineOfLane"}

