482:**Outbox publication (RULINGS Q38f).** deliveryKey: (id, publication sequence). sequence: durable per work-item id; increments when the payload to publish differs from the latest published payload for that id, so R1 to R2 to R1 publishes three deliveries (1650, 1700, 1650) and the receiver never suppresses the return. contentVersion: hashes (release, inputs, payload); recorded, never the delivery identity. atomicity: publication state (sequence and latest published payload) is persisted atomically with its outbox record. recovery: unacknowledged deliveries survive crashes and replay with their original identity. publishRule: per id the sequence starts at 1; publish (id, seq + 1) when the id was never published, when the payload's canonical bytes differ from the latest published payload, or when the id reappears after a recorded retirement (even with an unchanged payload); version is content identity only (RULINGS Q40c, Q40h). basisOrder: per subject the outbox applies an evaluation only if its basis is newer than the last applied one: greater basis.tx, or equal tx with a valid time not earlier; an older-basis evaluation publishes nothing (RULINGS Q40d). retirement: when a work item id disappears from a subject's outputs, the outbox records its retirement; a later reappearance publishes a new sequence even if the payload equals the last published one (RULINGS Q40h). liveOnly: a delivery's subject comes from the evaluation record; only live reconciles at the current basis are passed to publish; previews (GS.11) and past-valid-time audit evaluations never are (RULINGS Q44c). atomicUnit: per subject, the basis guard, retirement state, publication sequence, latest published payload and the outbox records are persisted in one atomic write under the subject's compare-and-set; the guard advances on empty and unchanged evaluations too (RULINGS Q42c). receivers: receivers apply deliveries per id by publication sequence: a state projection records the highest sequence applied and ignores any lower one (never regresses); an effect that needs every transition processes sequences in order and recovers gaps from the outbox before applying a later one; receiver deduplication is persisted atomically with the receiver's state or effect, or the recipient is idempotent by (id, seq); receivers exist from the first publication or are seeded with a state snapshot (RULINGS Q42b, Q44d). operations: W0 defines publish(state, evaluations) -> {deliveries, state} and receive(state, deliveries) -> state as pure operations so O-DOMAIN gives the expected outbox and receiver state (RULINGS Q40c, Q42b).
810:| CR-FAULT | fault-witness, case-result | VA | expected outcome chosen from the last witnessed transition in COMMIT_OUTCOMES (or the case's declared outcome), compared with final state, caller outcome, retries and post-recovery case results | every fault point injected at least T-FAULT-REPS times and every injection matches |
915:| B19 | success before publication | COMMIT_OUTCOMES.successRule, M-EARLY-ACK |
959:- **Outcomes.** The oracle derives each command's expected outcome from intent, order, preconditions, enforcement (CN-NEW-OR-WORSENED) and durable-decision evidence (COMMIT_OUTCOMES), then compares the receipts' outcomes afterwards. A receipt never decides which command is valid or committed.
