    22	
    23	### 1.1 Measurement semantics (v2, frozen before any comparison; Codex plan-v2 review findings 1 to 5)
    24	
    25	The bars above only count when measured under these definitions. A run that departs from them cannot close an NS obligation.
    26	
    27	**P1 pinned reads that never block writers.**
    28	1. Readers and writers share one hot set: at least 50% of reads target subjects written in the previous 60 s (section 2.1 active set).
    29	2. 10% of subgraph reads are issued as two or more queries at the same named basis and are scheduled to straddle a command the frozen
    30	   trace already holds for that subject: step 1 before that command's ACK, step 2 after it. The driver adds no writes of its own, so
    31	   faster readers never change the write load (Codex round 2, finding 19). The answer must equal the oracle's answer at the requested
    32	   basis, never the latest state.
    33	3. Every P1 window contains at least one maintenance event (XTDB compaction; QLever index rebuild or delta merge; index-layer catch-up).
    34	4. The driver records the basis it requested (from the receipts it observed) independently of anything the engine echoes; the verifier
    35	   checks every sampled answer at the driver-recorded basis.
    36	5. Writer waits caused by readers are attributed two ways: writer latency at each reader level against the zero-reader run on the same
    37	   trace segment and burst phase, and engine-side wait evidence where the engine exposes it (QLever broker queue wait; XTDB has no
    38	   read-path locks, so its evidence is the matched-phase comparison).
    51	
    52	**P3 change feed.**
    53	1. Commit time is the source of truth's publication instant for that transaction (the moment it becomes readable and the ACK may be
    54	   sent), stamped by the source on the host monotonic clock the driver also reads.
    55	2. The cursor is (epoch, position), a total order over transactions; per-subject order follows from it.
    56	3. The expected affected-subject set per transaction comes from the oracles: subjects written directly (O-REPLAY) plus subjects affected
    57	   through referenced entities under the world definition's dependency rules (O-DOMAIN, section 6.1).
    58	4. "Affected subjects known" means the consumer has durably recorded the affected-subject set and its checkpoint in one durable write.
    59	   Lag runs from commit time to that durable record.
